            <ArticleTitle>Hard truths and a new strategy for addressing childhood obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>106-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1089/chi.2011.0107</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We debunk three likely misperceptions about childhood obesity: (1) the epidemic thereof is caused by poverty, (2) information campaigns alone would be effective at reducing childhood obesity rates, and (3) obesity-reducing interventions would necessarily save money. We then discuss policies that could be effective at reducing childhood obesity rates and propose a tax/subsidy strategy that would provide the right incentives for governments, schools, and households to make appropriate investments in obesity prevention efforts.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
